<!--
  Pip, Claude Code Quest's mascot: one artboard holding the art, the state
  machine and its timelines (RML keeps an artboard's timelines and state
  machine inside its own element, so they share this file). The view model,
  its enum and the converters are in view-model.rml.

  Built only by `npm run pip:build` with the pinned Rive CLI. Script-free: no
  Luau, ever.

  The art is the approved board's front view, traced in an 800-unit reference
  space inside the root node, which scales it onto the 400 by 400 artboard.
  gazeX turns the head from the front view toward the board's three-quarter
  and side views: the Turn timeline, scrubbed by a joystick that follows the
  gaze plus the Body layer's own glances. The Turn and the resting values of
  every part it keys are derived from a box model, and only
  quest/client/pip/turn.ts (`npm run pip:turn`) writes them. Each moving part pivots at its own
  node: the neck (Head, Head Pose), the shoulders (Arm Left, Arm Right), the
  stalk bases (Antenna Left, Antenna Right), the soles (Foot Left, Foot Right),
  Hop for the whole body's lift and squash, and the outer edge of each sole for
  its lean (Lean Left, Lean Right), so a lean keeps a foot on the floor. Soft
  shading is feathered fills clipped to the part they shade.

  Three rules bind every timeline:
  - each Body timeline keys the whole rest pose, so any state blends back to it
    without a jump;
  - a blend state's exit names the timeline that times it (exitBlendAnimationId);
  - the Body layer never keys what the Turn timeline keys (the faces' spans and
    colours), so the turn and the poses add up.
  The Blink layer alone keys the eyes' height (a later layer wins), so the
  Reactions show surprise and joy through the mouth, the lids and the body,
  never the eyes' height.
-->
<Rive version="1" kind="fragment">
